        "thinking": "I see - `@paperclipai/db` exports `embedded-postgres` related things from the package level. The `api.ts` in the cloudflare worker imports from `@paperclipai/db` (for schema/type things), but the esbuild bundler then pulls in the entire `@paperclipai/db` package including the embedded-postgres exports.\n\nThe `hyperdrive.ts` file imports:\n```ts\nimport * as schema from \"../../../db/src/schema/index.js\";\nimport type { Db } from \"@paperclipai/db\";\n```\n\nThe direct schema import is fine. \n\nThe issue is that dynamic imports in the R2 upload handlers are pulling in the full `@paperclipai/db` package at runtime, which includes `embedded-postgres`. I need to mark `embedded-postgres` as external in the bundler config so it doesn't get bundled.\n\nChecking the Wrangler documentation, I'm realizing the configuration options for specifying externals aren't as straightforward as I initially thought—there's no direct `external` option in `wrangler.toml`, and I need to verify what the actual approach is in Wrangler 3. if `[esbuild_config]` is a supported section.\n\nActually, let me check if Wrangler supports a `define` config or if there's a special way to handle this.\n\nLooking at the Wrangler v3 docs, the key configuration options for bundling include:\n- `node_compat` (in wrangler.toml under `[build]`)\n- Custom build command\n\nBut I don't see a direct external option in wrangler.toml docs.\n\nThe cleanest solution here might be to:\n\n1. Mark `embedded-postgres` and `node:sqlite` as external via CLI: `--external embedded-postgres --external @embedded-postgres/* --external node:sqlite`\n\nBut we also need to handle the fact that this is called from wrangler dev. Wrangler supports passing esbuild options via the CLI `--var` but not externals directly.\n\nActually wait, I just remembered - in Wrangler v3+, there's support for `esbuild_compat_mode` and you can use `--no-bundle` for pre-built files. But more importantly, I should look at how Wrangler handles packages that contain Node-only code.\n\nA different approach would be to use a custom build script that bundles the worker with the right externals set, then run `wrangler dev --no-bundle` against the pre-built output. Or I could try `wrangler dev --external embedded-postgres --external @embedded-postgres/darwin-x64`, though wrangler doesn't directly expose esbuild externals as CLI flags.\n\nLet me check what wrangler v3 actually supports for configuration - I think there's a way to specify `[esbuild_config]` in a separate file.\n\nActually, the real issue is that `api.ts` dynamically imports from `@paperclipai/db`, which exports test utilities that depend on `embedded-postgres`. The cleanest fix would be to either separate those exports from the main index (better separation of concerns) or mark them as external in the bundle. The cleanest solution is to have the dynamic imports in the CF worker pull directly from the schema subpath rather than the top-level package export, which would avoid bundling the embedded-postgres dependencies. I could either use a subpackage export like `@paperclipai/db/schema` or follow the pattern already established in `hyperdrive.ts` with relative imports to the schema file. Another option worth exploring is marking embedded-postgres as external in the wrangler build configuration to prevent it from being bundled in the first place.\n\nLooking at what `api.ts` actually needs from the package, I should check whether it's just type imports or if there are runtime dependencies that require the full package.\n\nThe dynamic imports in the upload handlers are pulling in the table schemas, so I could point those directly to the schema file like `hyperdrive.ts` does instead of importing the whole package.\n\nBut there's also this `undici` and `node:sqlite` issue surfacing—`undici@7` introduced a SQLite cache store that requires the Node.js built-in `node:sqlite` module, and something in the dependency chain is pulling in a newer version. I need to figure out which dependency is bringing in this newer `undici` and whether I can constrain it or find an alternative.\n\nThe real problem is that `node:sqlite` is only available in Node 22.5+, and esbuild treats the conditional require as a hard dependency even though it's wrapped in a try/catch. For Wrangler, I can either fix the imports in `api.ts` to avoid pulling in `embedded-postgres` altogether, or add a custom build step to mark `node:sqlite` as external. The simplest path is probably just updating those two import statements to use the direct schema path instead. Looking at the error more carefully, I realize the `@embedded-postgres` packages are Node-only binaries that esbuild is trying to bundle, which won't work in Cloudflare Workers. Rather than wrestling with Wrangler configuration, let me try the direct approach: modify the imports in `api.ts` to prevent `embedded-postgres` from being pulled in, which should also resolve the `node:sqlite` issue coming from `undici`. Now I need to track down where `undici` is being pulled in and whether I can configure it as an external in the wrangler setup to prevent it from being bundled.",
